// Interference: why amplitudes are more fundamental than probabilities.
//
// Concept
//   Apply H, then Z, then H. After the first H the qubit is a 50/50
//   superposition. If it were merely "randomly 0 or 1", the second H would
//   leave it 50/50 as well. It does not: the result is deterministically |1>.
//
//   Z flips the *sign* of the |1> amplitude. The second H then has the two
//   paths cancel on |0> and reinforce on |1>. A classical coin cannot do this,
//   because probabilities cannot be negative.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   Deterministic |1>, up to a global phase.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `result` is 1 in 100% of shots. Compare with `superposition.qf`, which is
//   the same circuit without the Z.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   H Z H = X, since Z = H X H and H is its own inverse. Explicitly:
//   H|0> = (|0> + |1>)/sqrt 2, then Z gives (|0> - |1>)/sqrt 2, then H gives
//   |1>. The amplitude of |0> is (1/sqrt2)(1/sqrt2) + (-1/sqrt2)(1/sqrt2) = 0.
//
// @expect qubits=1 clbits=1 gates=3
// @expect-state |1>=1.0
// @expect-outcome 1

qubit q;
bit result;

h q;
z q;
h q;

measure q -> result;
